@meta
id: 10-15
title: The whole body, in three keys
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-brace, inner-vs-around
par: 96
minutes: 7
vimrc: set autoindent
vscode: click the first line of the body, shift-click the last, type
open: refund.provider.ts

@brief
When a brace pair opens and closes on the same line, `i{` is a run of
characters. When the braces sit on lines of their own — which is every
function body, every `if`, every `try` — vim quietly switches to whole
lines instead. `di{` takes out the lines between the braces and leaves the
braces where they are. `ci{` does the same and opens an empty line at the
right indent for you to type on.

That makes replacing a function body a three-key operation from anywhere
inside it. No selecting, no counting lines, no reaching the end of a
twenty-line method and wondering if you have gone one too far.

The legacy refund endpoint is gone. `RefundProvider.refund` should stop
talking HTTP and delegate to the payment service instead. The whole body
becomes one line:

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.total - order.discount);

The imports, the constructor and the class stay. The method keeps its
signature.

@keys
| `di{` | delete the lines between the braces |
| `ci{` | delete them and start typing at the right indent |
| `da{` | the braces as well, which is not what you want here |
| `iB` `cib` | the unshifted spellings |

@hints
You are inside the body already once you are on any line of it. The
object knows where the body ends.
--
`ci{` from inside the braces clears the body and leaves you in insert mode
on a blank, correctly indented line.
--
`11j` puts you inside the method. Then `ci{`, type the single line, `Esc`,
`:wq`.

@solution
    11j ci{ return this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.total - order.discount); <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Eighty-five keystrokes, and seventy-three of them are the line you had to
write anyway. The edit itself — finding the fourteen lines of dead body
and removing them — cost three.

This is the point in the chapter where text objects stop being a
convenience and start being the reason people use vim. The mouse version
of this edit is: click at the start of the second line, scroll, shift-click
at the end of the last line, check you have not caught the closing brace,
type. The vim version does not involve the word "check".

Two things to notice. First, the switch to linewise is automatic and is
based on the file, not on a setting: it happens when the braces are alone
on their lines. Second, `ci{` respects the indent, so the line you type
lands where Prettier would have put it.

`da{` is the version that takes the braces. On a function body that
leaves a dangling signature and is almost never what you meant — save it
for deleting a whole `if` block, where the braces should go too.

@start refund.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async refund(order: Order) {
    const amount = order.total - order.discount;
    const reference = `refund-${order.id}`;
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds/legacy', {
      amount,
      reference,
      orderId: order.id,
    });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}

@expected refund.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async refund(order: Order) {
    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.total - order.discount);
  }
}
